{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>These are the accountings of the Tabernacle, the Tabernacle of the Testimony.</b> The commentators disagreed about this matter: does 'these' refer to the making of the Tabernacle that he mentioned in the section of וַיַּקְהֵל, or does it refer to what he mentions in this section? And further they asked: why did Moses render an accounting of what was made from the silver and the copper, but did not render an accounting of what was made from the gold? Therefore my heart is moved to say that 'these are the accountings' refers to the making of the Tabernacle that he mentioned above, because they had already completed every work of the Tabernacle in general and in detail, and everything they needed to make from the silver and the copper — they had already made it all. Therefore Moses rejoiced that the work of the silver and the copper was completed, and he was eager to give an accounting to clear himself of suspicion. And he did not want to wait until after the priestly garments, which are mentioned in this section, were also made, and to give an accounting of the silver and the gold and the copper all together, because in everything mentioned in this section there was no silver and copper, and once the work of the silver and copper was already completed, he saw fit to give an accounting immediately. But concerning the gold he could not yet give an accounting, because they had not yet made all the priestly garments mentioned in this section, and those too contained gold. And since they still needed the gold, Moses could not yet give an accounting of the gold." "And if you will say: the question still remains — why did he not give an accounting of the gold after he had also completed the priestly garments? The answer to the matter is, because they said in the Midrash (Shemot Rabbah 51:6), which Rabbenu Bachaye brought in this section, and this is its language: When Moses came to give the accounting, there was missing from the weight one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five shekels, and he forgot and did not know what had been done with them. A bat kol went forth and said: 'וְאֶת הָאֶלֶף וּשְׁבַע הַמֵּאוֹת וַחֲמִשָּׁה וְשִׁבְעִים עָשָׂה וָוִים לָעַמּוּדִים' — to fulfill what is stated (Numbers 12:7) 'לֹא כֵן עַבְדִּי מֹשֶׁה בְּכָל בֵּיתִי נֶאֱמָן הוּא' — until here is its language. And it appears that the author of this midrash detected the two superfluous definite articles that are stated: 'וְאֶת הָאֶלֶף וּשְׁבַע הַמֵּאוֹת' — and it should have said 'וְאֶלֶף וּשְׁבַע מֵאוֹת,' just as he said above 'מְאַת כִּכַּר' and not 'הַמְּאַת כִּכַּר' with the definite article. And why did he mention the definite article with the thousand and the hundreds mentioned here? Rather, surely Moses forgot and did not know what had been done with them, and Israel were murmuring after him, saying that he had taken them for himself, and a bat kol went forth and said that that known thousand about which you spoke, and the known seven hundred about which you spoke, that Moses took them — it is not so; rather, he made them into hooks for the pillars. And if so, after Israel saw that heaven testified on his behalf, therefore they no longer wanted to receive from him an accounting of the gold, for they said: if he had withheld anything for himself from the gold, they would not have testified on his behalf from heaven even concerning the silver, since after all he would not have fulfilled the obligation of his accounting. And moreover, the bat kol says 'בְּכָל בֵּיתִי נֶאֱמָן הוּא' — the gold too is included; therefore he did not give an accounting of the gold." "And some say that even from the start they did not demand an accounting from Moses; rather, Moses on his own wanted to clear himself — therefore it is stated: <b>which was accounted by the mouth of Moses.</b> And as for what is written <b>the service of the Levites by the hand of Itamar</b>, some say that the nature of silver and gold is to diminish from its weight when one melts it; therefore he said here that everything accounted by the mouth of Moses is what was found in the hand of Itamar in its actual weight, and this was by way of miracle. And on account of this it was called <b>the Tabernacle of the Testimony</b>, for this is a testimony to Israel that the Shekhinah dwells among them." "<b>The Tabernacle, the Tabernacle of the Testimony.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tanhuma Pekudei 1) that it is for this reason that 'מִשְׁכָּן מִשְׁכַּן' is stated twice: because the Temple below is aligned opposite the Temple above, as it is stated (Exodus 15:17) 'מָכוֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ פָּעַלְתָּ ה׳' — מָכוֹן means 'aligned, this one opposite that one.' And in another midrash they said (Tanhuma Pekudei 2): מִשְׁכָּן מִשְׁכַּן twice — because it was taken down twice in Israel's sin. And for all the midrashim we need to give a reason what their relevance is here — for without doubt the main subject of this section is the accounting that Moses gave, so what is the relevance of these midrashim here?" "Therefore I say that all of them detected this difficulty: why did he insert the accounting in the middle of the work? For in the section of וַיַּקְהֵל the work of the Tabernacle and all its vessels is mentioned, and in this section nothing is mentioned except that he made the priestly garments — so why did he interrupt between them by inserting this accounting? Therefore the author of this midrash said: because the Temple below is aligned opposite the Temple of HaShem above, and it is written (Exodus 25:9) 'כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מַרְאֶה אוֹתְךָ אֵת תַּבְנִית הַמִּשְׁכָּן וְאֵת תַּבְנִית כָּל כֵּלָיו'. Behold, from here is proof that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not show Moses on the mountain anything except the Tabernacle itself and its vessels; but He did not show him the pattern of the priestly garments. For this reason it is stated several times in the section of תְּרוּמָה, 'כַּאֲשֶׁר הָרְאֵיתָ בָּהָר,' concerning the making of the Tabernacle itself and its vessels; but in the section of תְּצַוֶּה, where he mentioned the priestly garments, the vision on the mountain is not mentioned at all. And also, it was not for nothing that they were divided into two sections twice: for in the section of תְּרוּמָה the Tabernacle and its vessels are mentioned, and in the section of תְּצַוֶּה nothing is mentioned except the priestly garments. And so too here: the section of וַיַּקְהֵל speaks of the Tabernacle and its vessels, and the section of פְּקוּדֵי speaks of the priestly garments — because the Tabernacle and its vessels were all made in the vision that YHWH showed Moses on the mountain, and that vision was of the Temple above, for it too is fashioned after the pattern of this Tabernacle, and He commanded him to make a Tabernacle below that resembles in all its features the Temple above. And this is what he said: 'מָכוֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ' — aligned, this one opposite that one. But the meaning is not 'aligned opposite it' in an actual place, since the Tabernacle was portable and did not stand in one place; and if so we would have to say that the Temple above too was portable from place to place, and this is far from reason. And moreover, the language of the verse does not bear this out, for when he says 'מָכוֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ,' it implies that the House of HaShem below is aligned opposite the House of HaShem above — and the opposite should have been said: that the House of HaShem above is aligned opposite the House of HaShem below, since it was transported from place to place by means of the transport of the Temple below. Rather, 'מָכוֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ' means that the Temple below was made aligned after the pattern of the Temple above, which also contains the form of these chambers and vessels, and they are divine mysteries hidden from us. This is what he said: aligned, this one opposite that one. But the priestly garments below were made only for Aaron, for honor and splendor, and they are not included among the vessels of the Tabernacle. And when He said 'אֵת תַּבְנִית הַמִּשְׁכָּן וְאֵת תַּבְנִית כָּל כֵּלָיו,' the priestly garments are not included, for his garments are not called vessels of the Tabernacle but garments of Aaron, and they have no counterpart above." "And with this is settled what the commentators asked: why in this parashah, regarding the priestly garments, is it said 'in every respect as HaShem commanded,' and why was this not mentioned at all in the entire parashah of VaYakhel? Rather, it is because the Mishkan itself, whose pattern Moshe saw on the mountain — it is obvious that he made no change, for a thing that a person sees with his own senses is easy to replicate as a model. But the priestly garments, whose pattern he did not see — you might suppose that he forgot some detail about them. Therefore it says about all of them 'as HaShem commanded': even though he did not see their model, none the less, in all of them he intended to do as HaShem had commanded him, for there was nothing to them but the commandment and no visual form at all. But in parashat Terumah it mentioned in many places 'as He showed you on the mountain,' for the reason we have stated. And for this reason Moshe was most precise regarding the Mishkan and its vessels and wanted to give an accounting of them immediately after they were completed, and did not want to combine them with the making of the garments — because of the surpassing degree of holiness that he perceived in the Mishkan and its vessels, by virtue of its being aligned opposite the heavenly Temple above. And its explanation would be thus: <b>These are the accounts of the Mishkan,</b> — this demonstrative heh carries the sense that it speaks of the Mishkan on high. He returned and said, what is this? <b>The Mishkan of the Testimony, which was counted by the mouth of Moshe,</b> — meaning, that Mishkan of the Testimony which testifies that the Shekhinah dwells in Israel, which was counted by the mouth of Moshe — this itself is 'the accounts of the Mishkan' known above." "And according to the opinion of the Midrash that says 'Mishkan Mishkan' — because it was taken as a pledge twice on account of Israel's sin, therefore this is stated immediately after the account of the Mishkan was mentioned, because it says (Lamentations 4:11) 'HaShem has spent His wrath and kindled a fire in Zion.' Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Lamentations Rabbah 4:14): He spent His wrath upon wood and stones. They mentioned specifically wood and stones to indicate that it was precisely the Mishkan itself, which was made of wood and stones, that was taken as a pledge, but the priestly garments are not included. Therefore it mentions <b>These are the accounts of the Mishkan</b> immediately after the Mishkan, to hint that the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave them the sin of the Calf. For it is the way of the world: when a person is indebted to his fellow and gives him a pledge for his debt, he no longer collects the debt from his person but only from the pledge. And for the sin of the Calf, Israel incurred liability for annihilation in their very bodies, and the Holy One, blessed be He, in His mercy — may He be blessed — promised to accept a pledge from them for the debt. For the sin of the Calf, even though it was forgiven them, none the less it was forgiven only on condition — if they would not return to stray into the making of a calf. And when they did return to stray with the calves that Yerovam son of Nevat made, the sin of the first Calf was called to account upon them as well. And this is what it says (Exodus 32:34), 'And on the day of my visitation I will visit their sin upon them' — meaning, on the day that they make some other calf, that will be the cause to call to account and remember the deed of the first Calf, then 'I will visit their sin upon them' — to call to account upon them the sin of all the calves at once. But none the less I will not punish them in their bodies so as to annihilate them from the face of the earth; rather, I will take from them a pledge for their debt. And this is what it says: <b>These are the accounts of the Mishkan Mishkan</b> — for what I said, 'On the day of my visitation I will visit' — <b>these are the accounts</b> — meaning, that visitation shall fall upon <b>the Mishkan Mishkan</b>, corresponding to the two Temples that were destroyed on account of their sin. And it mentioned the heh in the first one and not in the second, because in the Second Temple five essential things were lacking, as is well known. And the reason for this pledge is that the gold of the Mishkan came to atone for the gold of the Calf, and if so, it is as though the Mishkan became a guarantor on their behalf, that they would not return to their straying. And if they do transgress, then I will take the Mishkan from them, for it is not fitting that the Mishkan, which atones between them, should remain while their impurity is still upon them and they return to their straying. And if so, all the while that the Mishkan stands, there is testimony for Israel that the sin of the Calf is permitted and forgiven; therefore it says <b>the Mishkan of the Testimony</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:20.915402646Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}